Accepted new evidence from wave 2, without scoring.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Justice Department Celebrates the One-Year Anniversary of the Executive Order on Competition","publisher":"U.S. Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-07-11","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/justice-department-celebrates-one-year-anniversary-executive-order-competition","claim":"DOJ said it recognized the first anniversary of Biden's competition executive order and described the antitrust division's interagency competition work as among its most productive recent years.","locator":"lines 131-139","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary DOJ press release describing the administration's competition agenda and implementation activity.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 policy year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Biden's executive order and administration policy direction.","contrary_evidence":["This is an institutional self-assessment, not an outcome study.","It does not quantify consumer or worker gains."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FTC Restores Rigorous Enforcement of Law Banning Unfair Methods of Competition","publisher":"Federal Trade Commission","publication_date":"2022-11-10","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2022/11/ftc-restores-rigorous-enforcement-law-banning-unfair-methods-competition","claim":"The FTC said it was restoring rigorous enforcement of the federal ban on unfair methods of competition and reactivating Section 5 as part of its mandate.","locator":"lines 358-361","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary agency statement on enforcement posture and competition policy.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 enforcement posture.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant to Biden-era antitrust leadership through FTC leadership choices and agenda.","contrary_evidence":["This is a policy statement, not an enforcement outcome.","It does not prove lower prices or higher welfare in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Federal Trade Commission and Justice Department Seek to Strengthen Enforcement Against Illegal Mergers","publisher":"Federal Trade Commission","publication_date":"2022-01-18","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2022/01/federal-trade-commission-justice-department-seek-strengthen-enforcement-against-illegal-mergers","claim":"FTC and DOJ launched a joint public inquiry to modernize merger guidelines and better detect and prevent anticompetitive deals.","locator":"lines 349-359","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary agency press release announcing a concrete antitrust process change.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and relevant to the year’s competition policy.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Biden's competition agenda, though the agencies are independent and outcomes depend on enforcement.","contrary_evidence":["A guideline review is not the same as stopping harmful mergers.","No direct output or consumer benefit is measured here."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DOT Announces Historic Bridge Investment Under Bipartisan Infrastructure Law","publisher":"U.S. Department of Transportation","publication_date":"2022-01-14","url":"https://www.transportation.gov/briefing-room/dot-announces-historic-bridge-investment-under-bipartisan-infrastructure-law","claim":"DOT launched the Bridge Formula Program, calling it the single largest dedicated bridge investment since the Interstate Highway System and allocating $26.5 billion over five years.","locator":"lines 139-141","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary federal implementation announcement with explicit funding amounts and program purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 infrastructure implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's signature infrastructure law, though execution is shared with DOT and FHWA.","contrary_evidence":["Funding announcement is not the same as completed infrastructure improvements.","Distributional effects are uneven and lagged."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treasury Seeks Public Input on Additional Clean Energy Tax Provisions of the Inflation Reduction Act","publisher":"U.S. Department of the Treasury","publication_date":"2022-11-03","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy1077","claim":"Treasury and IRS issued three additional notices requesting public input on clean-energy tax incentives and said about $270 billion of the IRA's climate investment flows through tax incentives.","locator":"lines 603-606","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary implementation statement from Treasury describing the scope of the IRA tax architecture.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 implementation work under the IRA.","ruler_attribution":"Directly connected to Biden's enacted agenda, though the statement is administrative and prospective.","contrary_evidence":["Public input is process evidence, not outcomes evidence.","It does not show realized household gains in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Internal Revenue Bulletin: 2022-43","publisher":"Internal Revenue Service","publication_date":"2022-10-24","url":"https://www.irs.gov/irb/2022-43_IRB","claim":"IRS notices requested comments on IRA implementation for clean vehicles, elective payment and credit transfer, and prevailing wage, apprenticeship, domestic content, and energy community requirements.","locator":"lines 339-354","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary tax-administration bulletin documenting concrete implementation steps under the IRA.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about late-2022 implementation of major 2022 legislation.","ruler_attribution":"Directly connected to Biden's legislation, with administration agencies carrying out the implementation.","contrary_evidence":["Comment requests are preparatory, not realized policy effects.","Administrative guidance does not itself prove economic benefit."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Fed - Overall Financial Well-Being","publisher":"Federal Reserve Board","publication_date":"2023-05-22","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/2023-economic-well-being-of-us-households-in-2022-overall-financial-well-being.htm","claim":"The Fed found that 73 percent of adults were doing at least okay financially at the end of 2022, down 5 points from 2021, with persistent gaps by education, race, and parental status.","locator":"lines 284-285, 294-303, 312-320, 346-347","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Fed household survey reporting direct self-assessed financial well-being and subgroup differences.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about household conditions in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual rather than directly presidential, because inflation and asset prices were major confounders.","contrary_evidence":["Strong labor markets coexisted with lower self-reported well-being.","Survey results do not isolate Biden-specific causation."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Fed - Income","publisher":"Federal Reserve Board","publication_date":"2023-05-22","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/2023-economic-well-being-of-us-households-in-2022-income.htm","claim":"The Fed reported that 33 percent of adults said family monthly income increased in 2022, but 40 percent said spending increased, leaving fewer adults with monthly budget margin.","locator":"lines 304-304, 336-336","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Fed household survey on income and spending changes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 household finances.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual because inflation and labor-market conditions are major confounders.","contrary_evidence":["This does not isolate the effect of Biden policies.","It captures perceived household change, not a causally decomposed policy estimate."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Labor force characteristics by race and ethnicity, 2022","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","publication_date":"2023-11-01","url":"https://www.bls.gov/opub/reports/race-and-ethnicity/2022/home.htm","claim":"BLS reported that in 2022 unemployment fell to 3.6 percent overall, while important gaps remained, including 6.1 percent for Black workers and lower employment-population ratios for Black adults than for White or Asian adults.","locator":"report summary and employment section, lines 269-270","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary labor-statistics report with direct subgroup labor-market measures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 labor market.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the macro recovery and policy mix, but not to the president alone.","contrary_evidence":["Labor gains were broad, but not equally distributed.","The report does not attribute subgroup gaps to Biden policy."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Personal Income by County and Metropolitan Area, 2022","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis","publication_date":"2023-11-16","url":"https://www.bea.gov/news/2023/personal-income-county-and-metropolitan-area-2022","claim":"BEA reported that personal income increased in 1,964 counties, decreased in 1,107, and was unchanged in 43 in 2022, with metro per-capita income up 1.7 percent and nonmetro up 1.2 percent.","locator":"lines 79-83","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary BEA geographic income release with direct county and metro coverage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 household income geography.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual because county income changes reflect broad economic forces and state/federal policy mixtures.","contrary_evidence":["Geographic spread was uneven and many counties declined.","County income growth does not isolate presidential causal contribution."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Price Adjustment Using Regional Price Parities","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis","publication_date":"2024-05-13","url":"https://apps.bea.gov/scb/issues/2024/05-may/0524-regional-price-parities.htm","claim":"BEA found 2022 state price levels ranged from 112.5 in California to 86.6 in Arkansas, showing large within-country differences in buying power.","locator":"lines 105-109, 117-128","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary BEA article documenting 2022 regional price parity estimates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022 household purchasing power and regional dispersion.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual rather than Biden-specific, but useful for adjusting household welfare by region.","contrary_evidence":["This measures price levels, not policy performance.","It does not indicate causation from federal policy."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Distribution of Personal Income","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis","publication_date":"2025-07-01","url":"https://www.bea.gov/index.php/data/special-topics/distribution-of-personal-income","claim":"BEA said its prototype distributional statistics cover 2012-2022 and measure how personal income is distributed across households, including quintiles and top/bottom 10 percent shares.","locator":"lines 110-114","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary BEA documentation for the distributional income series used to contextualize household incidence.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Methodology and series scope that includes 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Not ruler-specific; useful as a distributional measurement framework for the year.","contrary_evidence":["This is methodological context, not a 2022 policy outcome.","The page itself is a later documentation update."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Expiration of Pandemic Relief Led to Record Increases in Poverty and Child Poverty in 2022","publisher":"Center on Budget and Policy Priorities","publication_date":"2024-06-10","url":"https://www.cbpp.org/research/poverty-and-inequality/expiration-of-pandemic-relief-led-to-record-increases-in-poverty","claim":"CBPP concluded that the expiration of pandemic relief drove poverty back up in 2022 to 12.4 percent and led to a historic increase in child poverty, reversing gains from 2021.","locator":"lines 75-82","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable policy analysis using Census and Columbia data, but it is secondary analysis and explicitly interpretive.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 household welfare effects.","ruler_attribution":"Mixed: partly attributable to the end of temporary federal relief, but not uniquely to Biden in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["It is an interpretive policy analysis, not a primary statistical release.","The poverty increase reflects expiration of earlier relief as well as inflation."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National Survey: Families Faced Financial Strain When Expanded CTC Expired","publisher":"Urban Institute","publication_date":"2023-08-15","url":"https://www.urban.org/research/publication/national-survey-families-faced-financial-strain-when-expanded-ctc-expired","claim":"Urban found that families had a harder time affording essentials after the expanded Child Tax Credit expired, with more financial stress and food hardship.","locator":"lines 223-223","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable policy research organization, but this is a survey-based secondary analysis rather than a primary official release.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the post-expiration 2022 household period.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly related to Biden-era policy because the expanded credit was a federal policy that had expired by then.","contrary_evidence":["Survey timing matters and causality is not fully isolated.","The report is about the expiration effect, not a 2022 Biden action."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"How the American middle class has changed in the past five decades","publisher":"Pew Research Center","publication_date":"2022-04-20","url":"https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2022/04/20/how-the-american-middle-class-has-changed-in-the-past-five-decades/","claim":"Pew found the share of adults living in middle-class households fell from 61 percent in 1971 to 50 percent in 2021, with the upper-income share rising and the lower-income share also rising over the same period.","locator":"lines 142-147, 169-173, 188-190","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable research organization using government data; strong for long-run distributional context but not a Biden-specific 2022 effect.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Long-run context that frames 2022 but does not isolate that year.","ruler_attribution":"Not directly attributable to Biden; baseline context only.","contrary_evidence":["It is a long-run trend, not a 2022 policy result.","No causal attribution to the 2022 administration is made."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bumps, bipartisanship in long fight for semiconductor bill","publisher":"Associated Press","publication_date":"2022-08-09","url":"https://www.ap.org/news-highlights/spotlights/2022/bumps-bipartisanship-in-long-fight-for-semiconductor-bill/","claim":"AP reported that Biden administration aides viewed the CHIPS bill as economically and strategically important and believed its funds could help cut inflation and create new factory jobs.","locator":"lines 94-99","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable wire-style reporting on contemporaneous policymaker views, though still secondary and interpretive.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 CHIPS enactment period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Biden's industrial-policy agenda, but the claim reflects participant views rather than measured results.","contrary_evidence":["This is an elite interpretation, not an outcome study.","The bill's economic effects were prospective in August 2022."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"U.S. budget deficit halves to $1.375 trln despite $430 billion in student loan costs","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/us-fy-2022-budget-deficit-halves-to-1375-trln-despite-student-loan-costs-2918556","claim":"Reuters reported that the FY2022 deficit fell by half to $1.375 trillion, driven mainly by fading COVID relief spending and record revenues from a strong economy.","locator":"lines 409-409","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on official fiscal outturns, though the story itself is secondary.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about FY2022 fiscal outturn.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Biden-era fiscal posture and recovery, but heavily confounded by pandemic-era spending unwind.","contrary_evidence":["The improvement was driven mainly by the unwinding of emergency spending.","This does not prove broad household welfare gains."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement by Ambassador Katherine Tai Following Congressional Passage of the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022","publisher":"Office of the United States Trade Representative","publication_date":"2022-08-12","url":"https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2022/august/statement-ambassador-katherine-tai-following-congressional-passage-inflation-reduction-act-2022","claim":"USTR said the IRA would create good-paying jobs, lower health care and energy costs, reduce the deficit, and strengthen U.S. competitiveness.","locator":"lines 56-56","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official administration statement on intended economic effects, but not an independent outcome evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 enactment of the IRA.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's legislative agenda and administration framing.","contrary_evidence":["This is policy advocacy and intended effects, not observed outcomes.","The statement does not separate federal policy from broader macro conditions."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: How the Inflation Reduction Act Builds a Better Future for Young Americans","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-08-16","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-how-the-inflation-reduction-act-builds-better-future-for-young-americans","claim":"The White House said the IRA would lower the deficit, ask the wealthy and corporations to pay their fair share, and create a cleaner future with lower energy costs and good jobs.","locator":"lines 62-66","source_type":"other","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Secondary transcript of an official White House fact sheet; useful for policy intent but not an independent outcome record.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about the August 2022 enactment of the IRA.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's signed legislation and administration agenda.","contrary_evidence":["This is promotional language, not outcome data.","It does not establish that the predicted benefits materialized in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden-Harris Administration Makes $500 Million Available to Increase Innovative American-Made Fertilizer Production","publisher":"Farm Service Agency, U.S. Department of Agriculture","publication_date":"2022-09-28","url":"https://www.fsa.usda.gov/news-events/news/09-28-2022/biden-harris-administration-makes-500-million-available-increase","claim":"USDA said the Biden-Harris administration made $500 million in grants available to spur competition, combat fertilizer price hikes tied to Ukraine war shocks, and support independent American fertilizer production.","locator":"lines 169-174, 176-188, 203-205","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary agency release with concrete funding amounts, policy aims, and market-structure rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 crisis response and industrial support.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden-era executive branch action, though market conditions and the Ukraine war are major confounders.","contrary_evidence":["Price pressure was also driven by the war in Ukraine and global supply conditions.","The program is about support and competition, not proven 2022 results."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.6","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Fed - Overview","publisher":"Federal Reserve Board","publication_date":"2022-11-01","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/2022-november-financial-stability-report-overview.htm","claim":"The Fed said inflation remained unacceptably high in the United States, financial conditions had tightened, and household debt remained at modest levels relative to GDP.","locator":"lines 305-317","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Fed assessment of inflation, financial conditions, and household debt.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about late-2022 macro conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual because the Fed is independent and inflation was driven by many domestic and global factors.","contrary_evidence":["The report is not a presidential performance assessment.","Monetary tightening was set by the independent Fed."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9"]}
Search ledger: candidates found about 50, documents opened 27, duplicates or near-duplicates collapsed 16, irrelevant or out-of-period items 5, inaccessible items 4, accepted distinct URLs 20.

Material gaps and next best query/source directions:
- Direct 2022 appointment evidence is still thin. Best next sources: White House archive pages for Treasury, NEC, OMB, Commerce, and IRS leadership; Senate confirmation records; and official bios showing delegated authority.
- Distributional evidence for 2022 still leans on aggregate household and geography data. Best next sources: Census post-tax/disposable income tables, BEA distributional income series tables, and BLS wage percentile tables by race, sex, and region.
- More outcome evidence is needed to separate policy from recovery in 2022. Best next sources: later CBO/CMS/IRS follow-through on IRA implementation, CHIPS award announcements with independent evaluation, and empirical studies on inflation, real wages, and regional purchasing power.
- The record still lacks more independent analysis on whether Biden-specific actions improved market competition, household affordability, and medium-run investment conditions versus simply coinciding with a rebound.
